    "text": "An electron (mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math>) with an initial velocity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi>v</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mover><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mfenced></math> is moving in an electric field <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi>E</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mover><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mfenced></math> where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math> is constant. If at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>,&nbsp;de-Broglie wavelength is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></math>, then its de-Broglie wavelength after time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi></math> is given by",
